      { question: "Why must the dialysing fluid have the same osmotic pressure as blood?", answer: "If the dialysing fluid had a different osmotic pressure, water would move into or out of the blood by osmosis in an uncontrolled way; matching the osmotic pressure ensures only the intended wastes move across the membrane, not water." },
      { question: "Why must the dialysing fluid contain no nitrogenous wastes of its own?", answer: "Having no nitrogenous wastes in the dialysing fluid creates a concentration gradient, so the patient's nitrogenous wastes diffuse out of the blood into the fluid, purifying the blood." },
      { question: "State one key way this dialysis process differs from how a real, healthy kidney works.", answer: "A real kidney selectively reabsorbs useful substances (like glucose, amino acids, and needed water) back into the blood after filtration, while the dialysis machine performs no such reabsorption -- it simply lets wastes diffuse out." }
