      { question: "Describe the path blood takes through a mammal's heart in one complete circuit.", answer: "Deoxygenated blood is pumped from the heart to the lungs to be oxygenated, returns to the heart, and is then pumped out again to the rest of the body -- passing through the heart twice per cycle (double circulation)." },
      { question: "Why can a mammal not survive well with only a fish-like circulatory system?", answer: "Mammals need to maintain a constant body temperature, requiring a continuous, highly efficient oxygen supply from aerobic respiration; a single-circulation system without separated oxygenated/deoxygenated blood could not deliver oxygen efficiently enough to meet this demand." },
      { question: "What structural feature of the mammalian heart specifically prevents the mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood?", answer: "The septum, a dividing wall between the two sides of the four-chambered heart, keeps oxygenated and deoxygenated blood completely separate." }
